5. Facilitator Responsibilities
A Local Lab facilitator helps create the conditions for mathematical exploration.
The facilitator does not have to know every answer in advance. Instead, the role is to guide participants through the process of asking questions, investigating ideas, testing possibilities, and explaining their reasoning.
Before Each Session
1. Coordinate with the host
Confirm the reservation, access arrangements, room setup, supervision requirements, and any applicable policies.
2. Select the activity
Choose an investigation or mathematical topic that suits your audience.
You may use your own activity, adapt an existing resource, or request ready-made curriculum from Math Lab.
3. Prepare materials
Gather everything participants will need, including paper, writing utensils, manipulatives, printed instructions, or digital resources.
4. Plan the discussion
Prepare a few open-ended questions that encourage participants to investigate rather than simply search for a predetermined answer.
5. Prepare the room
Arrive early, arrange the tables and materials, and make sure the space is ready before participants arrive.
During Each Session
1. Welcome participants
Introduce yourself, explain the purpose of Math Lab, and establish a welcoming environment.
2. Introduce the investigation
Present the question or challenge clearly.
Allow participants time to think before explaining too much. An investigation is most useful when participants have room to develop their own ideas.
3. Encourage exploration
Ask questions, invite different approaches, and help participants investigate their ideas.
Avoid immediately giving away solutions when participants encounter difficulty.
4. Facilitate discussion
Encourage participants to explain their reasoning and compare strategies.
Make room for different mathematical backgrounds, communication styles, and approaches.
5. Record attendance
Maintain a basic attendance record in accordance with Math Lab and host requirements.
For minors, use appropriate procedures and avoid collecting unnecessary personal information.
6. Connect to the broader Math Lab network
When appropriate, discuss the current National Investigation and explain how participants can contribute to the group's solution.
Remember that Local Labs should spend at least a few sessions each month on the monthly National Investigation and submit a group solution to the Math Lab website.
After Each Session
1. Reflect
Record what worked well, which questions generated productive discussion, and where participants encountered difficulties.
2. Collect feedback
Ask participants what they learned, enjoyed, or found confusing.
3. Communicate next steps
Share information about future sessions with participants who have consented to receive updates.
4. Share progress
Complete the relevant feedback, reflection, or progress forms when available.
These records help Math Lab understand how Local Labs are developing and what support facilitators need.
5. Plan the next meeting
Use observations from the session to adjust the next activity, improve pacing, or explore questions that emerged during discussion.
