8. National Investigations
What Is a National Investigation?
The National Investigation is a monthly mathematical investigation that connects Local Labs through a shared question.
It provides an opportunity for participants in different communities to explore the same mathematical challenge, develop different approaches, and contribute to a broader mathematical discussion.
Local Labs are encouraged to use the National Investigation as a recurring part of their activities.
How Local Labs Participate
Step 1: Obtain the current investigation
Review the monthly investigation and its instructions using the official Math Lab website or the resources provided by the Math Lab team.
Make sure you understand the problem and any submission requirements before introducing it to participants.
Step 2: Introduce the problem
Present the investigation to your group.
Clarify any necessary definitions, but leave room for participants to interpret the problem, ask questions, and develop their own approaches.
Step 3: Investigate together
Allow participants to explore individually, in pairs, or in small groups.
Encourage them to:
* Try examples.
* Organize results.
* Identify patterns.
* Compare approaches.
* Test conjectures.
* Explain their reasoning.
Step 4: Discuss discoveries
Bring the group together to compare findings.
Different participants may reach the same result through different methods, or they may identify different aspects of the problem.
Use the discussion to develop a shared understanding of the investigation.
Step 5: Develop a group solution
The Local Lab should work toward a collective solution that explains the group's findings and reasoning.
A useful submission should communicate not only the answer, but also how the group reached it.
Where appropriate, include:
* The main result.
* An explanation of the method.
* Supporting examples or diagrams.
* A mathematical argument or proof.
* Important observations or extensions.
Follow the official submission instructions when they become available.
Step 6: Submit the solution
Local Labs should submit a group solution to the Math Lab website for the monthly National Investigation.
The submission process allows participants to contribute their work to the broader Math Lab community.
Check the official submission requirements for the appropriate format, deadline, and any other requested information.
Step 7: Share feedback
Facilitators can also communicate observations about the investigation to Math Lab.
Useful feedback includes:
* Which parts generated the most discussion.
* Where participants encountered difficulties.
* Which approaches were especially interesting.
* What extensions participants explored.
* Suggestions for improving future investigations.
This feedback can help the network improve its activities and better support Local Labs.
How Often Should Local Labs Work on National Investigations?
Local Labs should dedicate at least a few sessions per month to the current National Investigation.
The exact schedule can vary depending on the Local Lab's meeting frequency and participants' needs.
For example, a Lab that meets weekly might dedicate two meetings to the National Investigation and use its other meetings for geometry, puzzles, algebra, or other topics.
A Lab that meets less frequently can plan its monthly sessions to ensure that participants have sufficient time to investigate and prepare a group submission.
The important point is to make consistent progress on the shared investigation rather than treating it as an occasional afterthought.
Are National Investigations Mandatory for Every Session?
No. Local Labs have flexibility in choosing their activities.
Facilitators may explore any mathematical topic they wish, but each Local Lab should reserve a few sessions per month for the National Investigation and submit a group solution.
Participation in the broader National Investigation program is encouraged as a way to connect local mathematical discoveries with the Math Lab network.
Local Labs can continue to develop their own activities and identity while participating in the shared investigation.
